# Normalise line endings on commit so the working tree never shows phantom whole-file
# diffs. Without this, core.autocrlf alone leaves the stored form dependent on whoever
# happened to commit first.
* text=auto

# Source and project files: stored LF, checked out native.
*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.slnx      text
*.props     text
*.targets   text
*.json      text
*.xml       text
*.xaml      text
*.md        text
*.svg       text
*.manifest  text
*.rsp       text

# Native C++/WinRT half.
*.h         text
*.cpp       text
*.idl       text
*.vcxproj   text
*.filters   text

# Windows-only tooling that breaks on LF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# The mirror case: Unix-only tooling that breaks on CRLF, and it breaks confusingly.
#
# The ports are built on a Linux box (ports/ripcord-3ds/SETUP.md) while the tree is edited on Windows,
# where `* text=auto` checks these out native - so build-mbedtls.sh arrives with CRLF, `#!/bin/sh`
# is not an interpreter any kernel recognises, and the error names neither the file's real problem nor
# the line ending. GNU make is worse rather than better: it mostly tolerates CRLF, then passes the
# stray  into a recipe's shell, so the failure surfaces somewhere else entirely.
#
# This matters more than a checkout rule usually would because SETUP.md copies the tree with rsync
# rather than cloning it. rsync moves bytes, so whatever Windows checked out is what Linux runs.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.mk        text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch, never diff.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gz        binary
*.pcapng    binary
*.bin       binary
*.dll       binary
*.pkl       binary
*.saz       binary
